course: dictionary
lesson: 14092
title: Words about belonging
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Membership comes from the word member, someone who belongs to a club, group or organisation. When you see membership, look at the words around it for clues about which meaning fits. «Sam paid for his gym membership in January.» Paid and gym tell you membership is the right to use the gym, often bought with a fee.
explain: Membership can also mean how many members a group has. «The membership of the school choir grew to forty children.» Grew and forty are the clues. The choir did not get bigger in size of its songs; more people joined, so membership means the number of members.
explain: To check, put your meaning into the sentence in place of the hard word. «Ali's library membership lets him borrow ten books.» Try it: the right to use the library lets him borrow ten books. It still makes sense, so you have found the right meaning.
fact: Membership means being a member of a club, group or organisation.
fact: The membership is all the members of a group, or how many there are.
fact: A membership can be the right to use a club, gym or library, often paid for with a fee.
fact: In maths, you can compare the cost of a yearly membership with paying each time.
fact: Membership of a country in a group of nations means being one of the countries that belong to that group.
exercise: «Mia paid the fee for her swimming club membership.» What does membership mean here? | A swimming race | The deep end of the pool | *The right to use the swimming club, after paying | A wet towel
exercise: «The membership of the school choir grew to forty children.» What does membership mean here? | The teacher's name | The songs the choir sings | *All the members, or how many there are | A new room for practice
exercise: Which word is the noun for being a member of a club? | meeting | clubhouse | *membership | club
test: «Ali's library membership lets him borrow ten books at a time.» What does membership mean in this sentence? | Silence in a quiet room | A book about members | The number of books on the shelves | *The right to use the library
test: A gym membership costs £20 a month, and paying each time costs £5. Which is the best way to describe the membership? | *A paid right to use the gym for a set time | A race around the track | A free bag of gym clothes | A map of the gym
exam: Which sentence uses membership correctly? | Our membership is a green football kicked over the fence. | The membership rained heavily on the hill. | I membership my dinner with a fork. | *Our club has a membership of forty children.
